# YES(1)

## NAME

yes - print a string repeatedly

## SYNOPSIS

| **yes** [ string ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Yes* writes *string* followed by a newline to the standard output, over and over, until it is
stopped. With no argument the string is **y**.

It is a filter's answer to a program that asks questions: piping *yes* into a command supplies
the same reply to each of them.

**Note: this page is new.** *Yes* is one of the two commands in the v7 **/usr/src/cmd** tree
that came with no manual page anywhere in the system; the other is dmesg. The program is eight
lines and v7 evidently did not think it needed one.

## DIAGNOSTICS

None.

## BUGS

*Yes* has no terminating condition of its own. What normally stops it is the reader at the far
end of its pipe going away, which costs it a SIGPIPE; a *yes* whose output is a file or a
terminal runs until it is killed.

## SEE ALSO

kill(1), pipe(2), signal(2)
